|~ `::trace`, the kind of the trace a failure leaves, and what is declared
   under it: its constructor and the refusals of a payload that is no
   vector of frames, and the laws of a trace.
   The rules it holds: [QSERR#trace]. ~|

:trace
  |~~ A trace is what a failure leaves as it flies: a frame for each step
      that raised it or handed it on, from the step where it arose to the
      step where it is read. `[1 "x" 3] | map ~(mul 2) !| trace` answers
      the frame of `mul 2`, which failed on `"x"`, then the frame of `map`,
      whose code failed on the element at `/1`; a step the failure skips
      joins the steps of the last frame.

      An error holds it under `:trace`, written as the failure flies, and
      `trace` reads it from an error at rest; the verbs of vectors read
      it, `first` the frame where the failure arose and `last` the frame
      of the level that reads it. It prints a frame a line where it holds
      two or more, a frame standing on one line whatever its subject
      holds. It is the vector of its frames in the order the failure flew,
      `::trace[::frame["x" ~(add 1)]]`, and any other payload is refused.
      An error at rest written with frames under `:trace` resumes them
      when `raise` raises it, and an empty trace is none, the raise
      starting one at its own step.
      ::snippet~([1 "x" 3] | map ~(mul 2) !| trace)
      ::link~(::frame | doc) ::link~(::error/trace | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/errors | doc)
  ~~|
  ::kind
    | wraps
        |~~ The frames, from where the failure arose outward, a vector of
            frames plain or under `::trace`, built into the trace, a frame
            under a tag read as the frame beneath it.
        ~~|
    | throws :PayloadKindNotTakenError
        |~~ A trace is the vector of its frames in the order the failure
            flew, `::trace[::frame["x" ~(add 1)]]`: a payload of another
            kind is refused, a set, which holds its frames in the one order
            of values, and a vector under a tag of another kind among them,
            whose frames `payload` reads first.
        ~~|
    | throws :ElementKindNotTakenError
        |~~ Every element of a trace is a frame, `::frame["x" ~(add 1)]`;
            `:index` names the first that is not, and `:expected` the kind
            of a frame.
        ~~|
    | proves :holdsAFrameForEachStep
        |~~ A failure inside the code of `map` holds the frame of the step
            that failed and the frame of `map`.
        ~~|
        ~([1 "x" 3] | map ~(mul 2) !| trace | count | eq 2)
    | proves :joinsTheStepsSkippedToTheLastFrame
        |~~ A step the failure skips joins the steps of the last frame. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 | mul 2 | count !| trace | last | skipped | eq ~(mul 2 | count))
    | proves :printsAFrameALine
        |~~ A trace of two frames prints a frame a line. ~~|
        ~([1 "x" 3] | map ~(mul 2) !| trace | print | lines | count | eq 4)
    | proves :resumesWhenRaised
        |~~ An error at rest written with frames resumes them when raised.
        ~~|
        ~(!{:a 1 :trace ::trace[::frame[1 ~(pre)]]} | raise | count !| trace | eq ::trace[::frame[1 ~(pre | count)]])
    | proves :takesAPlainVectorOfFrames
        |~~ A plain vector of frames is a trace. ~~|
        ~([::frame[1 ~(pre)]] | tag ::trace | eq ::trace[::frame[1 ~(pre)]])
    | proves :readsAFrameUnderATag
        |~~ A frame under a tag is read as the frame beneath it. ~~|
        ~(::trace[::X::frame[1 ~(pre)]] | eq ::trace[::frame[1 ~(pre)]])
    | proves :PayloadKindNotTakenError/refusesASet
        |~~ A set of frames is no trace, written or raised. ~~|
        ~(get [~(::trace#[::frame[1 ~(a)]] !| type) ~({:trace #[::frame[1 ~(a)]]} | raise !| type)]
          | eq [::trace/PayloadKindNotTakenError ::trace/PayloadKindNotTakenError])
    | proves :PayloadKindNotTakenError/refusesAVectorUnderAnotherTag
        |~~ A vector of frames under a tag of another kind is refused. ~~|
        ~([::frame[1 ~(a)]] | tag ::T | tag ::trace !| type | eq ::trace/PayloadKindNotTakenError)
    | proves :PayloadKindNotTakenError/refusesANumberARaiseLays
        |~~ A number a map writes as its trace is refused when it is
            raised.
        ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :trace 5} | raise !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::trace/PayloadKindNotTakenError ::number])
    | proves :ElementKindNotTakenError/refusesAMap
        |~~ An element that is no frame is refused at its index. ~~|
        ~(::trace[{:failed ~(add 1)}] !| get [~(type) /index] | eq [::trace/ElementKindNotTakenError 0])
    | impl ::builtin{}
